1. We first sorted the fields by their threat level. The order from highest to lowest is:
  • Field_4 (threat level 0.11, full protection requires 3 drones)
  • Field_3 (threat level 0.10, full protection requires 2 drones)
  • Field_2 (threat level 0.07, full protection requires 4 drones)
  • Field_1 (threat level 0.06, full protection requires 4 drones)

2. For the most threatened field, Field_4, we checked its status. It already has 3 drones protecting it (Drone_1, Drone_2, and Drone_3) so no additional drone is needed there.

3. We then set aside the drones already protecting Field_4. The remaining drones are Drone_4 (currently assigned to Field_2) and Drone_5, Drone_6, Drone_7, Drone_8 (currently protecting Field_1).

4. Although we were instructed to sort the remaining drones by their distance to Field_4, no additional drone needed to go to Field_4 since it is already fully covered.

5. In order to minimize overall damage, we now “redistribute” the remaining drones to cover the other fields – giving priority to the higher-threat fields while using our limited fleet:
  • Field_3 is next in the threat ranking and requires 2 drones. Looking at the positions relative to Field_3’s center, it makes sense to assign the drones that are best positioned. Both Drone_4 (originally protecting Field_2) and Drone_8 (originally protecting Field_1) are relatively closer to Field_3 and are thus assigned there.
