Below is the reasoning that led to this assignment:

1. First the fields were sorted by threat level descending. The threat levels are:
  • Field_2: 0.20 (most threatened)
  • Field_3: 0.17
  • Field_1: 0.09
  • Field_4: 0.04

2. For the most threatened field – Field_2 – the full protection requires 4 drones. It already has 4 drones (Drone_2, Drone_4, Drone_5, and Drone_6) protecting it. So no extra drones need to be added there, and those drones are locked in to continue protecting Field_2.

3. Next, the remaining drones (those not protecting Field_2) are considered. They are initially Drone_1, Drone_3, Drone_7 and Drone_8. (In step 4 the drones would be sorted by their distance to Field_2; the two originally assigned to Field_3 have the closest positions relative to Field_2 – roughly 17 units away – while Drone_8 and Drone_7 come next.)

4. Now following threat‐order for the other fields, the next highest threat is Field_3 (0.17) and its full protection requires 2 drones. Accordingly, the two nearest remaining drones – Drone_1 and Drone_3 – are kept protecting Field_3.

5. Finally, of the drones left (Drone_8 and Drone_7 in that sorted order), the next field in threat order is Field_1 (0.09; note that although Field_1 needs 4 drones to be fully protected, it has none yet, and its threat level is higher than Field_4’s 0.04). So Drone_8 is assigned to Field_1 first and then Drone_7 is also assigned to Field_1.

6. Field_4, though it originally had protection, is not among the higher–threat fields once Field_2 and Field_3 are taken care of and with only 2 drones available to distribute, the algorithm gives priority to the next most threatened Field_1.

Thus, the final assignment is as shown above.
